    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of every layer and participates in every forward pass.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts*; tokens are routed to whichever device owns the relevant expert.\n\nThese are orthogonal and frequently combined (TP within a node, EP across nodes), alongside data parallelism for request-level replication. This note covers the mechanics of both, their data flow, the collectives they rely on, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer with attention and an MLP block. For a TP degree of $T$:\n\n**Attention:**\n- Query, key, value projections $W_Q, W_K, W_D \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$ are split **column-wise**: each rank holds $d/T$ output channels. This means each rank computes attention for a distinct subset of heads (assuming $d/T$ is a multiple of head_dim).\n- Output projection $W_O$ is split **row-wise**: each rank holds $d \\times d/T$.\n\n**MLP:**\n- First (gate/up) projections split column-wise: each rank holds a slice of the intermediate dimension $d_{ff}/T$.\n- Second (down) projection split row-wise.\n\n**Embeddings and LM head** are typically split along the vocabulary dimension, with an all-reduce (or all-gather) to assemble logits.\n\n### 2.2 Data flow",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
